#
# EAS boost for foreground apps.
#
# Without it the UI visibly stutters. Measured with dumpsys gfxinfo on a scroll
# through Settings, same sequence, two runs each:
#
#                     without        with
#   janky frames       28.4%         0.4%
#   median frame       19 ms         11 ms      (60 Hz budget: 16.6 ms)
#   99th percentile    32 ms         22 ms
#
# The reason is a mismatch between framework and kernel. This MediaTek kernel
# has SchedTune (CONFIG_SCHED_TUNE=y) and does not expose uclamp in the cgroups:
# /dev/cpuctl/*/cpu.uclamp.min does not exist. Up to Android 11 AOSP set the
# /dev/stune boosts inside init.rc; in Android 12 those lines were dropped
# because it moved to uclamp, and only one remains, for nnapi-hal.
#
# The result: apps do land in the right group -- task_profiles_29.json does
# that, and it is what the system loads since the vendor is API 29
# (MaxPerformance -> JoinCgroup schedtune top-app) -- but that group stays at
# boost=0 and prefer_idle=0, that is, with no effect at all. Nobody raises the
# frequency for what the user is looking at, and nobody prefers the idle cores.
#
# Why a service and not "write" lines in here: tried, they do not work. With
# "on post-fs-data" or "on property:sys.boot_completed=1" init does run the
# lines (verified with a sentinel setprop) but only prefer_idle sticks, while
# boost goes back to zero. Applied five seconds later they stay.
#
# To be revisited if we ever move to a kernel with uclamp in the cgroups: there
# the framework handles it by itself and this file has to go.

service s88pro_schedtune /system/bin/sh /system/etc/s88pro-schedtune.sh
    user root
    group root system
    disabled
    oneshot

on property:sys.boot_completed=1
    start s88pro_schedtune
